These automated build and test processes are executed frequently so that integration problems are detected early.
Experienced development teams who have embraced the concept of CI cannot imagine undertaking any sizable software project without it. The practice of CI has enjoyed a significant uptake in adoption rate by the software development industry since the early 2000s, thanks in large part to the pioneering efforts of individuals such as Martin Fowler 2 and Kent Beck.
In the coming years, the practice of CI will be recognized for its huge potential when applied to modern BI development environments. BI ecosystems are inherently complex (see figure 1).
Multiple upstream data sources. ETL processes periodically extract, clean and load data from each of these upstream sources into data marts or data warehouses.
Here are a few real-world examples:
A change in a view in a DB causes a dramatic increase in report runtimes.
So how can the principles of CI be applied to a typical Business Intelligence project?
Automated execution of test cases for each artifact. These test cases can be used to ensure such things as:
Automated consistency checking. For each artifact:
Tracking of BI ecosystem changes so that when a test starts to fail, project stakeholders have a clear view of “who’s changed what” since the last cycle. For example:
What artifacts have been changed (and by whom?)
Have there been drastic changes to the amounts of data in the relevant data sources?
The BI team gains back countless hours otherwise spent manually testing all artifacts to make sure something hasn’t broken, saving time, but also maintaining momentum (it allows BI authors to focus on real development tasks).
2 Martin Fowler’s original paper describing Continuous Integration was published in September of 2000.
