Previously, we discussed the “out of scope” label together with disabling the test cases. During the repair, the report authors have the following actions available:
The part of the repair phase where the team is constantly executing the circle of “develop, test, develop, test” is commonly the most intense part of the project. This requires a lot of time, and progress is not always easy to manage.
Capture the changes in production and label them “changes week X for sandbox.”
Rerun the test cases and see if the result has changed or if there is a negative impact. Version control and Diff functions in MotioCI help the report author easily find the differences and the updates to make.
Depending on the size and number of reports, the project team should start this process some days before the “go live” moment. Copy the MotioCI test cases in the Sandbox to the live environments by drag-and-drop and ensure that a backup of the content store has been made.
Assess the results and decide on “going live.”
After a successful upgrade, the BI program focus changes from managing the upgrade itself to ongoing maintenance and monitoring of the new BI environment.
Business Intelligence (BI) however, has been slower to adopt testing as an integrated part of development in BI software such as IBM Cognos. Let’s explore why BI has been slower to adopt testing practices and the consequences ofNOTtesting.
BI projects are under constant pressure to be delivered faster. What some organizations may not realize is that the easiest phase to reduce time is testing.
Faster is better. This is not necessarily an “agile” approach and may only get you to the wrong place quicker.
This is similar to the previous bullet. The thinking is that “our users will test it.” This approach can lead to unhappy users and lots of support tickets.
Poor architecture may not be discovered if testing is ignored. Design issues can contribute to usability, performance, re-use, as well as, maintenance and upkeep.
Decisions made on bad data may be devastating to the business. There’s nothing worse than trying to manage by metrics that are based on incorrect information.
Now that we’ve laid out why organizations might not be testing and the pitfalls that occur when you do not test BI, let’s look at some studies on testing in software development. Studies Show Testing Your BI Platform Saves Money!
A paper on successful software development tacticsdemonstrates that most errors are made early in the development cycle and that the longer you wait to detect and correct, the higher it costs you to fix. So, it doesn’t take a rocket scientist to draw the obvious conclusion that the sooner errors are discovered and fixed, the better.
And once the system is in the operation phase,the relative cost to correct the error has risen to 98, nearly 100 times the cost of correcting the error if found in the requirements phase!
