Anthropology: The Indigenous Veddas of Sri Lanka
‘Veddas’ literally means ‘people of the forest.’ According to studies, they are the earliest inhabitants of the island and have lived there before any other ethnic groups arrived. However, today, the Veddas are also the last ethnic tribe in Sri Lanka.
During their earlier times, when the tribe was thriving, they roamed the Great Plains in the north central regions of the central mountains, but today, what remains of the tribe is now confined to Dambana, close to Mahiyangana and the Maduru oya Sanctuary. Now, the Veddas’ are on the brink of extinction.
It was also where one of the most important and sacred Buddhist temples, the Mahiyangana Raja Maha Vihara, was built. When the number of Sinhalese villages started increasing in the area, most of the Veddas adopted many of their modern customs and lifestyle, thus endangering their own indigenous lifestyle.
