15 useful tips to combat writers’ block
1) Use reading prompts to combat writers’ block
2) Joining a writer’s group online
Although it may sound silly to look around the room at your current surroundings. You might ask yourself, how is looking around the room going to help me combat writers’ block?
By looking around the room and focusing on one particular object, you have found your next topic to write about.
Whatever the memories are, write about them as they are still flowing into your mind. Write a poem, a short story, or even a book if you’re feeling really inspired, and just keep on writing …
Maybe it’s a beautiful maple tree with its colorful branches reaching up to the sky. Why not write a poem about the beauty you see exploding from this magnificent maple tree?
Write about them.
5) Tuning in to your senses
All authors struggle from time to time with writer’s block. Another valuable tip to try is tuning into your own senses to come up with fresh topics to write about.
Here’s the solution…
Or maybe it’s the scent of wildflowers, or freshly cut grass blowing in through your open window?
Listen, what do you hear?
Or maybe nothing?
Sometimes, reading a book can inspire ideas to use in your own writing. Keep your journal nearby when you’re reading so that if something in the book inspires a writing idea, you can jot it down.
Better yet, what’s better to help with relieving writers’ block than re-reading one of your absolute favorite childhood books?
Re-reading a favorite children’s book can help combat writers’ block. Are You There God?
8) Buy some sticky notes
You can do it! Use Your Creativity
ThankYou for My Dream Job of Being an Outstanding Writer!
Another wonderful idea to help combat writers’ block is to check out other authors’ websites. While you are browsing their work, something might help to give you a writing idea for your own next piece.
10) Watch some book trailers on YouTube and get inspired
Book trailers are a great way to help combat writer’s block and may help to give you some fresh new writing ideas. There are some pretty outstanding book trailers out there.
Many local book clubs have weekly or monthly social gatherings. Members can get together over coffee and snacks to trade books and discuss their current reading projects.
Create a public writers’ group on Facebook and watch your group grow. It’s surprising how many other book lovers and struggling authors are out there.
Freewriting courses are a great opportunity to learn new styles of writing and genres to help master your craft. You can also browse other people’s writing samples to help get inspired.
13) Go to the local library
There are so many books and genres to choose from. Where do you start?
Pick an appealing cover that interests you. Find a quiet spot in the library, and begin reading!
You might find a few splendid books hidden amongst the dusty aisles to help inspire your creativity and combat writer’s block.
15) Write, write and write some more
The best way to combat writers’ block is to keep on writing.
Get ready to jot down ideas as they come to you. Unleash the author inside of you and get writing — what are you waiting for?
