COVID-19 brings up the need for UVC
COVID-19 has brought many economic activities to a halt, and just as humans are beginning to adapt to the new normal, one of the old applications has come alive like a rebirth. It is ultraviolet light (UV), and in particular the bacteria-killing C-band (UVC) of ultraviolet light at 200-280nm.
IP Rating:
Appraisers use them to detect the authenticity of antiques. There are many pigments that now contain phosphorus, which glows under black light, while most ancient pigments do not contain phosphorus.
Maintenance personnel use them to find invisible cracks in the machine - by injecting a small amount of fluorescent dye into the fuel supply and illuminating it with black light;
Law enforcement officials can use them to identify the authenticity of money. The United States and many other countries have used an invisible fluorescent strip on their large denominations, which are only shown under black light.
Forensic scientists use them to analyze the crime scene. In order to capture fingerprints, for example, they are usually sprinkled with a fluorescent dye under a black light.
