What are China policies to support the vigorous development of the printing and packaging industry?
As the paper printing and packaging industry has a relatively strong ability to absorb labor, and the degree of environmental pollution is relatively low, the national and local governments have strongly supported it. In recent years, the Chinese government has issued a large number of industrial policies related to the paper printing and packaging industry.
In October 2011, the former General Administration of Press and Publication and the Ministry of Environmental Protection issued the “Announcement on the Implementation of Green Printing” and decided to jointly implement green printing. The scope of implementation includes printing production equipment, raw and auxiliary materials, production processes and publications , Packaging and decoration and other printed matters, involving the entire production process of printed products.
Enterprises should not purchase products that do not meet the requirements of the competent commercial authorities to prevent excessive packaging and promote recycling.
In May 2015, the State Council issued the “Made in China 2025″ strategic plan. “Made in China 2025″ is a national strategic plan to strengthen high-end manufacturing, and is the first decade of action in the “Three Decades” strategy of building China as a manufacturing power.
It is necessary to focus on the development of intelligent equipment and intelligent products, promote the intelligentization of production processes, cultivate new production methods, and comprehensively improve the intelligent level of enterprise research and development, production, management and services. In the future, with the continuous popularization of smart manufacturing, smart packaging and printing will become the future development direction of the industry.
In July 2016, the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology and the Ministry of Finance jointly issued the “Notice of the Volatile Organic Compound Reduction Plan for Key Industries.” According to the target requirements of the Plan, by 2018, the VOCs emissions of the industrial sector will be reduced by 3.3 million tons compared with 2015.
The “Plan” clearly stated that the packaging and printing industry should implement process technology transformation projects, and promote the application of low (no) VOCs content green inks, varnishes, fountain solutions, cleaning agents, adhesives, thinners and other raw and auxiliary materials; Encourage the use of flexographic printing technology and solvent-free composite technology, and gradually reduce the gravure printing technology and dry composite technology.
“Guiding Opinions on Accelerating the Transformation and Development of my country’s Packaging Industry”
