There are no restrictions on exposure to asbestos. It can happen almost anywhere, mainly due to the use of asbestos everywhere for decades.
A regular column in a New York newspaper discussed the topic of vigorous cleaning of asbestos roof tiles. The topic comes from a reader’s question, and architect Monte Leeper made his warning suggestions.
Electric roof tiles are one of the more common home decoration projects that can expose people to asbestos. Since asbestos causes mesothelioma, homeowners should consider health issues before hiring a company or performing tasks in person.
According to The Spruce website, American roofing material manufacturers (such as Johns-Mansville) provide at least one roofing ribbon tile product including asbestos cement. By the 1930s, asbestos had become the main product of the roofing industry.
Getting companies to change their business practices is the biggest obstacle to phasing out asbestos. This substance was still a feature of new houses until the 1970s and 1980s, when the public and the medical community’s outcry against alternatives finally defeated the indifference of greedy American companies to protect people from health hazards.
Leeper wrote: “It seems harmless to spray water into the water, but under high pressure, it is like hitting the surface with a bullet.” This bullet force can damage the material, thereby destroying asbestos roof tiles.
Leeper explained that asbestos tiles must be cleaned with a “soft brush” like a car cleaning brush. You can also use a brush to apply detergent, such as dishwashing liquid.
You should contact the testing company to collect air samples at home and outdoors. We recommend that you do this before and after hiring someone to safely remove asbestos from the roof.
